Dear Rafael,

the Acer Swift 3 SF314-56G with NVIDIA GeForce MX250 (bios V1.14)=20
suffers from an ACPI power resource regression where the power resource=20
controlling the dGPU is incorrectly marked as unused and turned off=20
during initialization.
This causes the GPU to enter D3cold state, making it inaccessible to the=
=20
NVIDIA driver.

The symptom was:
nvidia 0000:02:00.0: Unable to change power state from D3cold to D0,=20
device inaccessible

This commit adds a DMI quirk to prevent the power resource from being
turned off, similar to the existing Thunderobot ZERO quirk (cd7ef20ba8c6e9=
).

Testing and Validation
=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D=3D

I have manually performed the following tests on my Acer Swift 3 SF314-56G=
:
- Kernel parameter tests as potential workarounds
- Git bisect between kernel 5.15 and 5.16 to identify the problematic comm=
it
- Revert tests on multiple kernel versions (5.16, 6.1, 6.8, 7.0.2, 7.2.3)
- Testing the proposed quirk patch on kernel 7.2.3-1-cachyos and kernel=20
7.6.2 vanilla (with localmodconfig)

All tests were performed manually on my hardware. The patch has been
verified to work correctly on my system.

Important Note on Kernel 7.x:
When attempting to revert commit a1224f34d72a on kernel versions 7.x and=
=20
newer, significant merge conflicts occur in drivers/acpi/power.c. While=20
these can be resolved manually, the quirk patch approach avoids these=20
conflicts entirely and is therefore the cleaner solution.

I am not a developer, but this is my first contribution to the kernel=20
community. I have used an AI tool to assist with the analysis and=20
interpretation of system logs, but all testing was performed manually by=
=20
me. The patch works for me, but please be critical about the=20
interpretation and patch file. I am available for further testing and=20
